  "query_preview": "..\nHigh pressure is currently centered just to the \neast over southern New Mexico with PWAT values right around\nnormal for this time of year. Thunderstorm activity will be\nsubdued again today thanks to the proximity of the ridge. Hi-res\nguidance shows isolated activity mostly south and west of\nTucson as well as the White Mountains. The main threat will be \noutflow winds to 40 mph with maybe a couple gusts to 50 mph in \nthe stronger storms and locally heavy rainfall. \n\nWith the ridge almost directly overhead temperatures warm\nfurther today and Saturday with highs running 8-12 degrees above\nnormal. The Tucson metro area has a 30-70 percent chance to hit\n110 degrees both Friday and Saturday with areas further west\nlike Ajo and Organ Pipe National Monument seeing 15-30 percent\nchances to hit 115 degrees Saturday. Areas south and east of\nTucson will see highs 100-105 degrees. Low temperatures will\nalso be on the warmer side generally above 80 degrees from\nTucson westward thanks in part to dewpoints around 60 degrees.\nThis will lead to widespread areas of Major HeatRisk and areas\nof Extreme HeatRisk in the Safford area, Tucson metro, and\nwestern/central Pima County. Because of this an Extreme Heat\nWarning is in effect Friday and Saturday. \n\nSaturday expect an uptick in thunderstorm chances and coverage\nas the ridge moves northward and becomes centered near the Four\nCorners. PWAT values will range from 1.2\"-1.5\" with plenty of\ninstability in the afternoon and evening. Models also show\nmodest mid level flow of 15-20 knots at 500 mb. Storms likely\nform first over the higher terrain and sky islands during the\nfirst part of the afternoon then move into the lower deserts\nwith storm coverage increasing late afternoon into the evening\nfrom central Pima County eastward. Storms will be capable of\nstrong to severe wind gusts with blowing dust a possible issue\nalong I-10 in Pinal County. SPC has most of southeast Arizona\nunder a marginal risk for severe wind gusts Saturday. Heavy\nrainfall will also be a threat with localized flash flooding\npossible. \n\nMid-level winds weaken some Sunday into Monday, but still expect\ndaily thunderstorm activity with the high centered over the Four\nCorners. Expect normal monsoon hazards from storms with strong\ngusty outflow winds and locally heavy rainfall. High\ntemperatures also begin to decrease Sunday falling to around\nnormal Monday into Wednesday. Ensembles show a modest increase\nin mid-level flow Tuesday and Wednesday which could lead to an\nincreased threat of strong to severe thunderstorms. Regardless\nfrom Saturday into next week expect daily monsoon thunderstorm\nactivity with normal varying degrees of coverage and intensity\neach day.",
